OVERVIEW OF THE DIFFERENCES BETWEEN SNIP AND SPIN

SNIP uses a specification language that  is very close to Promela, and thus
shares some similarities with SPIN which are discussed here.


----[ 1. Overview ]--------------------------------------------------------

First  and foremost,  SNIP is  about model  checking behaviour  models with
features. Features are Boolean parameters of a model.  In the case of SNIP,
the  features can  control  whether  or not  some  statement  in the  model
should be executed.  Consider the following model, for instance:

 i = 0;
 i++;
 assert(i==1);

We might want to  make the "i++" statement optional.  The way of doing this
is to introduce a boolean feature variable, say "Increment", which controls
the whether or not "i++" will be executed:

 bool Increment;
 int i = 0;
 if :: Increment -> i++;
    :: else      -> skip;
    fi;
 assert(i==1);

This model can then be instantiated for each valuation of the feature para-
meter.  In general, a behavioural model with n features can be instantiated
in 2^n different ways.

SNIP will check  that all those instances satisfy a  property (LTL, assert,
absence of deadlocks). SPIN, in contrast, will only check one instance, the
one  corresponding to  the initial  values of  the feature  variables. When
checking the assertion in the example,  SNIP will tell that it is satisfied
if feature  Increment is  true. SPIN  will say that  it is  always violated
since it only considers the case in which Increment is false.

SNIP has  to be able to  tell the difference between  feature variables and
normal boolean  variables.  Therefore, all features have to be  declared as
fields of a type named "features":

 typedef features {
   bool Increment
 };

In addition, those features have to be defined in a feature model, that is,
a model that records which combinations of features are valid.  The feature
model is to be provided in a separate file, using the TVL syntax (for this, 
see  http://www.info.fundp.ac.be/~acs/tvl).  The code fragment given before
then becomes the following:

 features f;
 int i = 0;
 gd :: f.Increment -> i++;
    :: else        -> skip;
    dg;
 assert(i==1);

The gd statement works like an if statement; "gd" stands for "guard".


----[ 2. Difference in Promela syntax ]------------------------------------

The language  of SNIP is called fPromela.  fPromela is a  (large) subset of
Promela,  supporting  most of the constructs.  Notable exceptions are those
for trace  assertions,  simulation and embedded C code.  A detailed list of
the non-supported keywords is given below.  

As hinted at before, the type "features" is not a regular type, but treated
differently by SNIP.  The fields of this type (which  we will call "feature
variables" hereafter) have to be Boolean variables.

Feature variables can only be used inside the conditions of  guard (dg..gd)
statements.  It is not allowed to mix feature variables and other variables
in the condition of a gd  statement.  A correct example is given above.  An 
incorrect example would be:

 features f;
 int i = 0;
 gd :: i == 0 && f.Increment -> i++;
    :: else                  -> skip;
    dg;
 assert(i==1);

The gd on line three contains feature variables and normal variables, which
will result in a parse error of SNIP.

It is possible to use if..fi instead of gd..dg. SNIP is able to distinguish
an if statement  from a guard by looking at the types of the variables.  If
all variables in the condition are feature variables, the if..fi is treated
as if it were a gd..dg.



----[ 3. Difference in Promela semantics ]---------------------------------

In a  nutshell, one fPromela model  is semantically equivalent to  the non-
deterministic choice between  2^n Promela models (where n is  the number of
features) that can be obtained by varying the initial values of the feature
variables.

More formally, a proctype in fPromela does not define a normal finite state
automaton, but  a featured finite  state automaton.  That is, one  in which
each transition  is not only labelled  with a Promela expression,  but also
with a  "feature expression",  i.e. a Boolean  expression over  the feature
variables.  These expressions are obtained from the gd statements.  Whereas
executing a Promela specification will yield a transition system, executing 
an fPromela  specification yields a  featured transition  system.  See also 
http://www.info.fundp.ac.be/~acs/fts.

SNIP performs  verification on  the featured automaton  instead of  the 2^n
automata that can  be derived from it.  Thus, the feature variables are not
part of  the system state.  Instead the information about features  is kept
symbolically, as Boolean functions (normally BDDs) attached to each state.


----[ 4. Difference in functionality ]-------------------------------------

SNIP  covers the  main verification  scenarios that  also SPIN  covers, but
extends them to fPromela: checking never claims, assertions and deadlocks.

SNIP will not automatically report  non-progress cycles. This can easily be
achieved with  an LTL  property and  the np_ variable.  SNIP also  does not
support the checking of trace assertions.  In SNIP,  atomic blocks are only
atomic wrt. other processes, not wrt. the never claim.

Of course, SNIP has also lots  of features that SPIN does not have, and the
command-line interface is quite different (and self-documented).


----[ 5. Unsupported Promela keywords ]------------------------------------

There are only a few language features of Promela that are not supported by
SNIP. These  are listed below,  grouped by  the likelihood of  being imple-
mented in  the future.  This list will  be updated as  SNIP is  updated and
items might move from "might be" to "will be".

Will be implemented:
- Unsigned data types.
- The "np_" variable (global variable true in a non-progress state).
- pc_value(x) (returns the current location of the process with pid x).
- Inline calls (can use preprocessor instead).
- Recognise states labelled with "end" as valid final states.

Might be implemented:
- The 'd_step' keyword (one can use atomic instead).
- Sorted send '!!' and random receive '??'.
- Channel polls (angle brackets around receive parameters: q?<eval(y),x>).
- For simulation and debugging: 
  - d_proctype (deterministic proctypes)
  - The 'priority' keyword for proctype declarations (to influence 
    selection of which process to advance when simulating).
  - rand (random number generation)
- The 'hidden' keyword for variable declarations (excludes a variable from 
  the system state).
- The 'provided' keyword for proctype declarations (setting a global
  constraint on process execution; adds this constraint to the 
  executability condition of all its transitions).
- The 'local' keyword for variable declarations (only to be implemented 
  when partial order reductions are implemented is used).
- Escape sequences (the 'unless' keyword).
- Channel assertions ('xs' and 'xr').
- Remote referencing of variables with 'proc[pid]:var'.
- Remote referencing of labels with '@'.
- Trace assertions ('trace' and 'notrace').
- Reading input (the 'STDIN' channel).

Unlikely to be implemented:
- Embedded C code.
- The 'show' keyword (only useful for XSPIN).
